ms. Another comment explains that stage call-sites must never override this idleTimeoutSeconds themselves, because harness.ts's invocation logic prefers a call-site-supplied value over the executor's default, and if a stage baked in its own constant that would let a "live" value silently override a value that was supposed to be frozen as part of a run's configuration snapshot. Everything in this sandbox object is described as being part of a canonical JSON comparison performed elsewhere (requireFrozenBinding) so that changing any field here invalidates any run that was frozen with the old configuration; the file's comment warns to only change these values when no run is in progress.

harness.ts is the lowest-level file: it contains the actual subprocess management logic used to run an executor once, plus a couple of supporting utilities. It defines InvocationInput (the prompt text, optional idle/absolute timeout overrides, an optional model string, and an optional working directory), HarnessOutcome (everything about how the process run went: the raw stdout, stderr, exit code, duration, whether it timed out, any spawn or kill error strings, and a flag for whether the output was truncated because it exceeded a size cap), and HarnessEnvelope (the parsed, meaningful fields extracted from the JSON envelope that Claude Code prints — effective model, a fallback model field, token counts in and out, cache read/write token counts, cost, and the actual result text). Two byte-size constants, PROMPT_MAX_BYTES and RESULT_MAX_BYTES, are both set to 1MB, with a comment noting these will eventually move to configuration but for now live here as the single place to change them.

The file exports several functions. killTree(pid) is a small cross-platform utility to kill an entire process tree rather than just the immediate child process: on Windows it shells out to taskkill.exe (located via SystemRoot rather than relying on PATH, since the sandboxed environment doesn't guarantee one) with /pid, /t (tree), and /f (force) flags; on POSIX it sends SIGKILL to the negative of the pid, which targets the whole process group, falling back to killing just the pid if that fails. This exists because a runaway child process (or grandchild) that survives a naive single-process kill would defeat the timeout mechanism entirely.

probeExecutor(executor, options) runs the executor's probe command (typically something like claude --version) synchronously via spawnSync, to verify up front that the executable actually resolves and works in the environment that will be used to spawn it — the file calls this "hazard 9," the idea being to fail fast and loud with a clear error rather than have the real invocation fail mysteriously later. It supports overriding the executable path (which must be absolute), a timeout, a custom environment, and a working directory, and it throws descriptive errors distinguishing a timeout, a spawn failure, and a non-zero exit code (including any captured stderr/stdout in the message).

buildHarnessEnvironment(executor, source) constructs the actual environment object handed to the child process by copying over only the variables named in the executor's envPassthrough list from a source (defaulting to the real process.env). This is the enforcement point for the "named variables only" environment policy described in executor.ts.

parseEnvelope(executor, raw) parses the raw JSON text produced by the harness into the structured HarnessEnvelope. It documents that its field names were derived from one actual recorded invocation rather than from external documentation, which is treated as a hard rule elsewhere in the project. The trickiest piece of logic here is determining "effectiveModel": Claude Code's envelope apparently includes a modelUsage map that can contain multiple models (for example, an auxiliary title-generation call alongside the real turn), and the top-level usage.input_tokens field corresponds only to the real, effective model's usage. So the function finds whichever single entry in modelUsage has an inputTokens value matching the top-level usage.input_tokens, and only assigns effectiveModel if there's exactly one such match; otherwise it's null. All numeric fields default to null rather than zero when absent, preserving the distinction between "not reported" and "reported as zero."

invokeHarness(executor, input) is the core piece: it actually spawns the child process and returns a Promise that always resolves (never rejects) so that callers can handle every outcome — including spawn failures, timeouts, and overflow — uniformly and always retain evidence. It builds the environment via buildHarnessEnvironment, appends --model to the argv if a model was requested, and spawns the executor's command with shell:false (to avoid any shell-parsing ambiguity, directly invoking the native executable) and with stdin/stdout/stderr all piped. On non-Windows it spawns detached so the process group can be killed together; Windows relies on taskkill /t instead. It wires up two independent timers: an idle timer that resets every time any stdout or stderr data arrives, and an absolute timer that is set once and never reset — either one firing invokes the same fireTimeout logic, which kills the process tree and marks timedOut. stdout is accumulated into a buffer array, but capped at RESULT_MAX_BYTES: once the cap is hit, further data is silently dropped, resultOverflow is set to true, and the timeout/kill path is triggered immediately as a way to abort an oversized response early while still keeping the truncated prefix for diagnosis. Output is buffered as raw Buffer chunks and only decoded to a string once at settle time, specifically so that multi-byte UTF-8 characters that happen to be split across two separate pipe "data" events aren't corrupted. There's careful defensive handling around process lifecycle events: an 'exit' handler records the exit code and, because a descendant process that inherited the stdout pipe could otherwise prevent 'close' from ever firing (which would hang the whole run while holding a repository lock), it waits a short grace period (CLOSE_GRACE_MS, 1 second) and then settles anyway if 'close' hasn't happened; a 'close' handler settles immediately; an 'error' handler records a spawn error and settles; and an 'error' listener on stdin swallows EPIPE/EOF errors that occur if the child exits before consuming its input, preventing an unhandled error from crashing the whole Node process. Finally, the prompt is written to the child's stdin and stdin is closed immediately, which the comments emphasize is a deliberate design choice — the prompt travels over stdin, never as a command-line argument, presumably to avoid argv length limits and history/log leakage of prompt contents.

dispatch.ts sits above harness.ts and executor.ts and orchestrates one complete, auditable invocation attempt. It defines DispatchInput (a stage id, agent name, role, requested model, prompt text, and optional invocation overrides) and a DispatchResult discriminated union that is either a success carrying an agentRunId and the parsed envelope, or a failure carrying a human-readable reason string. The exported dispatchOnce function is described as implementing an "evidence-safe order," fixed as a critical review finding: probe the executor, invoke it, retain the raw output, write an audit log entry, and only then branch into success or failure — ensuring that no failure path can occur without leaving a recorded trace. It first looks up the stage from a Store (imported from ./store.ts, not read here) and fails immediately if it doesn't exist; then checks the prompt doesn't exceed PROMPT_MAX_BYTES; then calls probeExecutor and returns a failure result if that throws. It then calls invokeHarness with the prompt, requested model, and any invocation overrides. Immediately afterward — before any parsing or success/failure branching — it calls writeRawOutput (from ./raw-output.ts) to persist the raw stdout to disk keyed by the run's root directory and run id, and does the same for stderr if present, prefixed with a "--- stderr ---" marker; a comment labels this as addressing "hazard 2," ensuring raw evidence is retained no matter what happens next. It defines a local failed(summary) helper that writes an agent.dispatch.failed audit event via appendAudit (from ./audit.ts) and returns the failure result, then uses this helper to handle, in order: a spawn error, a result-size overflow, a timeout (including any kill error in the message), and a non-zero exit code. If the process succeeded, it attempts to parse the envelope via parseEnvelope, again using the failed helper if parsing throws. On full success, it inserts a new row into the store via insertAgentRun, capturing the stage id, agent, role, executor id, requested and effective models, fallback, token/cache counts, cost, duration, and — importantly — SHA-256 hashes of both the input prompt and the raw output (computed via the local sha256 helper built on node:crypto), plus the reference to where the raw output was written and a hardcoded independence value of "configured_standalone". It then appends a successful agent.dispatch audit event and returns the success result with the new row's id and the parsed envelope.

In short, executor.ts is pure configuration/policy — what to run and how tightly to restrict it; harness.ts is the low-level, general-purpose subprocess runner that enforces timeouts, output caps, and tree-kill semantics while resolving to a rich outcome object under all circumstances; and dispatch.ts is the stage-aware orchestration layer that ties a specific pipeline stage and store to one harness invocation, guaranteeing that every attempt — successful or not — leaves behind raw output on disk, a hashed audit trail, and, for successes, a structured database record. The files depend on each other in a strict layered fashion (dispatch imports from harness and executor; harness imports the executor's type; executor imports only a path-prefix constant), and the recurring theme throughout is defensive, evidence-preserving design: nothing is trusted to succeed cleanly, and every failure mode has both a defined behavior and a recorded trace.","ttft_ms":1111,"type":"result","duration_ms":44225,"uuid":"7b9600ab-7779-4d77-80e8-76c7ca76cdce","ttft_stream_ms":539,"time_to_request_ms":71,"first_content_frame_ms":539,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
